VGE shader ownership and engine separation
==========================================

Source proposal
  docs/Rendering.ShaderOwnership.Proposal.md is authoritative.
  Status: planning only; all implementation and acceptance tasks remain unchecked.

Scope and shared contracts
  Establish IGpuProgram with default implementation methods, implemented by independent
  GpuProgram and renamed GpuComputeProgram families. Remove VGE executable dependence on
  engine ShaderProgram inheritance, IShaderProgram, ShaderRegistry and CurrentShaderProgram.
  Establish explicit VGE declaration, preparation, activation, reload and retirement ownership.
  Resolve the cached pbr_final registration failure without constructing placeholder engine stages.

  Preserve StateCache, GraphicsCommandContext, GraphicsPipeline, EngineBoundaryScope and existing
  program scopes. No shared GpuShader base, parallel activation stack, current-owner dictionary,
  second context registry, per-frame native scans or blanket invalidation.
  Keep IShaderSubmissionTarget narrow; keep mutable state in concrete owners, not the interface.
  Reuse SPIR-V loading, binary caches, ShaderLinkBatch, generated bindings and resource deletion.
  Keep compute feature-owned where appropriate; do not force global compute registration.

  Non-goals: replace terrain rendering, engine traversal/culling, framebuffer allocation, shader
  compilation tools or native shader patching; support arbitrary engine-shader interruption;
  merge graphics and compute preparation backends or rename every feature shader class.
  Engine integration stays in adapters. Borrowed resources keep their existing ownership.

Execution and evidence
  Phases are dependency-ordered review increments of one migration. Intermediate states are not
  independently shippable while liquid inputs, reload invalidation or retirement remain incomplete.
  Temporary compatibility paths must have removal tasks below; none may become a second authority.
  Run every build/test tool through a subagent as required by repository instructions.
  Keep source/installed-IL evidence, compilation, focused unit tests, headless GPU integration,
  packaged-shader verification and user-run game acceptance distinct. Do not launch the game.
  Record concise receipts with the relevant task and linked contracts; no completion marks based
  solely on source inspection where executable or live evidence is required.

Decisions to resolve before dependent implementation
  ☐ Choose internal/public IGpuProgram accessibility and restricted state/hook access.
    - Account for public GpuProgram and internal IShaderSubmissionTarget/GpuProgramLayout.
      Do not expose mutable lifecycle state or internal layout solely to enable default methods.
  ☐ Specify distinct default workflow entry points, family hooks and required concrete facades.
    - Prevent same-slot recursive dispatch; preserve protected Submit and authored overrides.
  ☐ Select explicit compute reload policy: retain feature-owned reconstruction initially, or
    implement retained rebuild descriptions and transactional replacement before promising it.
    - RetryPreparation alone is not invalidation of an already valid compute executable.
  ☐ Choose the liquid pool interception after verifying installed call sites: narrow input adapter
    at selected getter calls, or direct translation of the supported uniform operations.
  ☐ Specify safe-boundary rejection/defer policy for actively borrowed executable replacement,
    duplicate declarations, registry lifetime and actual shutdown/context-deletion ordering.

Phase 1: Pin ownership contracts and migration coverage
  Outcome: interfaces, call-site obligations and validation boundaries are concrete enough to migrate.
  Proposal: Evidence and current dependencies; Proposed ownership model; Compatibility.

  ☐ Inventory inherited API and ambient ownership consumers.
    - Trace GpuProgram partials, GpuComputeShader, GpuShaderPrograms, GraphicsCommandContext,
      EngineBoundaryExecution, direct Use/TryUse/UseScope and low-level compute consumers.
    - Include LiquidGraphicsSubmission, MeshDataPoolManager.Render, LiquidPoolSubmissionHook,
      SceneColorProgramBindings, EngineRenderScopes and test/diagnostic uniform adapters.
    - Audit OwnedShaderSubmissionHook/GpuProgramStopHook and automatic frame/light inputs;
      distinguish VGE compensation from native terrain/atmosphere/material/particle behavior.
  ☐ Confirm installed engine registration, Use/Stop/Dispose, pool input/finally restoration and
    reload call ordering against current assembly/API evidence, without running the game.
  ☐ Define declaration identity, installed executable revision/asset generation and originating
    native context identity using RenderContextRegistry; forbid numeric-ID-only ownership.
  ☐ Resolve the decisions above in the proposal or linked owning contracts before their consumers.
    - Define binding-only restoration versus immediate input replay and next-draw publication.
    - State which incoming engine/raw/compute footprints remain unsupported at boundaries.
  ☐ Map existing tests to interface dispatch, cache loading, scopes, generators, liquids, registry,
    reload and disposal; identify fixtures that currently hide registration side effects.
  ☐ Completion: each inherited/ambient dependency has a replacement owner or retained integration
    role, open decisions have an execution gate, and the migration has a source-backed test matrix.

Phase 2: Establish IGpuProgram workflows and compute naming
  Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: shared behavior and generated owners have a verified VGE contract.
  Proposal: Shared interface and default implementation; Compute family rename and migration.

  ☐ Introduce IGpuProgram default workflows for guarded preparation, activation/publication,
    scoped use and terminal lifetime; reuse the narrow IShaderSubmissionTarget view.
    - Keep lifecycle flags and CPU-block ownership in stable per-owner state with restricted access.
      Keep settings, retained generated values and installed generations with their family owners.
    - Separate workflow methods from preparation, Submit and disposal hooks; retain required
      concrete facades and bridge protected Submit without recursive interface dispatch.
    - Check guards before cleanup-owning activation; a rejected recursive call must not unwind
      an enclosing submission. Publish inputs even when the program ID is already bound.
    - Make retirement idempotent; reject mutation/disposal during publication and prevent revival.
    - Share CPU uniform registration/write guards and terminal release; preserve extra-resource
      disposal hooks, failure cleanup and compute diagnostic Dispatch overrides.
  ☐ Rename GpuComputeShader and its file to GpuComputeProgram; update family constructors,
    derived declarations, type references, fixtures, tests and documentation references.
    - Keep GpuComputePipeline as the single native compute executable owner and preserve direct
      and indirect dispatch contracts. Do not add common-workflow double deletion.
  ☐ Update RuntimeSubmissionEmitter, BindingReader and InterfaceBindingReader recognition.
    - Resolve the existing pipeline-field assumption deliberately; preserve concrete eligibility,
      abstract intermediate families, authored Submit overrides and property mutation guards.
    - Verify runtime/offline generated shells without assuming default methods become class members.
  ☐ Validate through concrete, IGpuProgram and IDisposable entry points: sequencing, failed
    publication cleanup, repeated disposal, recursion rejection, owned CPU blocks and generated inputs.
    - Keep current family preparation differences explicit; no compute invalidation no-op contract.
    - If graphics still needs an engine transition path before Phase 4, keep it temporary and
      explicitly isolate/remove it in Phases 3-4 rather than weaken the final interface contract.
  ☐ Completion: generated families and focused dispatch/lifetime checks pass; compute family naming
    is migrated, native ownership is singular, and remaining engine transition dependencies are listed.

Phase 3: Migrate existing scopes and liquid input translation
  Depends on Phase 2. Outcome: VGE use/restoration and liquid input staging no longer need an engine owner.
  Proposal: Existing scope ownership and engine handoff; Liquid mesh-pool input bridge.

  ☐ Consolidate graphics/compute restoration within existing program scopes and command lifetimes.
    - Replace CurrentShaderProgram-based VGE capture and compute numeric-ID owner lookup with
      borrowed IGpuProgram/generation references carried only where the existing scopes need them.
    - Preserve GraphicsCommandContext ShaderActivation, pipeline-change/EndPass cleanup and
      EngineBoundaryScope exceptional cleanup; do not duplicate selected-pipeline ownership.
    - Validate context, revision and retirement before restoration; stale tokens must not unbind
      another owner or restore a recycled handle. Extend existing scopes only as required.
    - Restore input resources where immediate scopes promise it; otherwise preserve explicit
      next-draw submission. Avoid redundant replay across complete resource-restoring boundaries.
    - Route direct Use/TryUse/UseScope and low-level compute paths through their declared scope
      contract; retain standalone/headless operation without a game singleton.
  ☐ Move native shader capture/Stop/Use to Rendering/Integration at supported outer boundaries.
    - Include native restoration effects in the footprint and ordered cleanup; preserve rejection
      of unsupported native/raw/compute incoming states and targeted StateCache mutation semantics.
  ☐ Implement the chosen narrow MeshDataPoolManager input bridge during LiquidGraphicsSubmission.
    - Translate origin, modelViewMatrix and forcedTransparency into the typed liquid input sink.
      Preserve engine culling, dimension offsets, interpolation, visibility and finally writes.
    - Keep pool draws on GraphicsCommandContext.Draw and borrowed geometry lifetime/validation
      with EngineLiquidPoolGeometry and LiquidGraphicsSubmission.
    - Native pool rendering must still receive the native shader; handle unknown inputs explicitly.
      No global CurrentActiveShader patch or engine executable proxy. An input-only adapter must
      reject unsupported executable operations and never enter engine registry/current-owner state.
  ☐ Validate all graphics/compute nesting combinations, unchanged-ID submission, success/failure,
    exceptional cleanup, resource restoration, raw bindings, disposal and context/ID reuse.
  ☐ Validate visible/depth liquids for ordinary, empty/culled pools, dimension offsets,
    mini-dimension transforms, preview transparency, restoration writes and exceptions; include
    unrelated native pools and real engine traversal where feasible.
  ☐ Completion: boundary and liquid checks pass; VGE scope behavior is independent of engine owner
    state, with native handoff contained in integration. Remove temporary scope compatibility paths.

Phase 4: Remove engine executable inheritance and registration
  Depends on Phase 3. Outcome: independent VGE executables prepare and render on cached/uncached paths.
  Proposal: Executable preparation and retirement; Proposed ownership model; Compatibility.

  ☐ Remove ShaderProgram/ShaderProgramBase inheritance and IShaderProgram from VGE executable owners.
    - Remove engine Shader creation, stage slots, EngineDisposed reflection, PassId and inherited
      uniformLocations requirements. Keep useful GpuProgramInterface diagnostic projections.
    - Remove RegisterOnPreparation/RegisterMemoryShaderProgram coupling and transition paths.
    - Move liquid interface methods fully into the bridge; migrate remaining uniform/texture
      consumers and fixtures to generated properties or preparation-owned numeric helpers.
  ☐ Compose graphics executable ownership through GpuProgramObject.Adopt and GpuResource policy.
    - Prepare/validate candidates before publishing executable, layout, installed settings,
      graphics identity, asset generation and successful revision together.
    - Retain the previous generation after failure/superseded requests without claiming readiness
      for incompatible requests; reject stale GraphicsPipeline borrowers after replacement.
    - Preserve ShaderLinkBatch, SPIR-V loader, binary cache, numeric bindings and desired inputs.
    - Update PendingShaderProgram transfers for synchronous, batch, failed and cancelled paths;
      detach/release temporary stages once after successful linking. Cached programs need no stages.
    - Use StateCache.DeleteProgram/targeted resource retirement; reject or defer active borrowing
      according to the chosen policy. Keep terminal disposal distinct from executable invalidation.
  ☐ Expose preparation diagnostics so mandatory HDR failure identifies the program and real cause.
  ☐ Remove obsolete OwnedShaderSubmissionHook/GpuProgramStopHook behavior after explicit frame/light
    bindings are verified; reconcile SceneColorProgramBindings, LiquidPoolSubmissionHook and
    EngineBoundaryExecution type checks. Preserve native-only hooks and profiling labels via adapters.
  ☐ Validate pbr_final from a real executable-cache hit with no NewShader or engine registration;
    repeat cache miss, invalid-cache fallback, batched load and preparation failure.
    - Test replacement failure/supersession, monotonic successful revisions, cancellation and
      exactly-once stage/program retirement. Fixtures must reject accidental engine registration.
  ☐ Completion: structural checks and focused GPU evidence show independent cached/uncached rendering,
    no engine executable assignability/registration, and no stale engine-specific VGE hooks remain.
    Reload/shutdown completion in Phase 5 is required before release.

Phase 5: Establish registry, reload and context lifetime
  Depends on Phase 4. Outcome: VGE ownership remains correct across assets, worlds and context retirement.
  Proposal: Concrete registry responsibilities; Reload and lifecycle sequencing.

  ☐ Make GpuShaderPrograms an instance owned by client rendering lifetime with explicit composition.
    - Decouple core identity from ICoreClientAPI; preserve typed lookup and useful mismatch diagnostics.
    - Enforce the selected duplicate-declaration policy without silently discarding incompatible
      types/contracts/settings or constructing throwaway replacement owners on asset reload.
    - Keep declarations/lookup free of binary reads and GL work; selected preload follows configuration.
      Preserve demand preparation, failed-input suppression and observable readiness/failure details.
    - Own removal, shutdown and callback invalidation so retired worlds/registries cannot revive.
  ☐ Adapt assets/logging through ShaderAssetReader, digest access and diagnostic sinks at integration.
    - Include ShaderLinkBatch's engine asset-manager boundary; preserve shared source identity and
      asset generation without duplicating import/digest/binary-cache services.
  ☐ Replace delayed RegisterAll invalidation with a synchronous reload transaction.
    - At a safe boundary withdraw old readiness, mark reload, clear digest/import caches and capture
      coherent settings. Let the engine refresh/rebuild native shaders before completing VGE invalidation.
    - Withdraw dependent render publications, reopen demand preparation and preload only selected
      mandatory sets when configured. Handle native exceptions, suppressed reload and compile failure.
    - Report native API reload status separately from VGE lazy/mandatory readiness; eliminate any
      window where old assets appear current because a callback has not executed yet.
  ☐ Apply the chosen compute reload policy to feature owners and test successful-executable reload.
    - Preserve CPU input state required by the policy; do not treat failure retry as executable reload.
  ☐ Wire actual shutdown order: stop submissions, dispose renderer/PSO borrowers, retire registry
    programs and drain native deletion on the live originating context.
    - Keep configuration, asset reload, world leave/re-entry and native context retirement distinct.
      Withdraw owner knowledge before context reuse; abandon deletion with a destroyed context
      rather than delete a matching numeric handle on another context.
  ☐ Validate duplicate/mismatched declarations, demand/preload failures, reload ordering/failures,
    settings edits, feature compute reload, world re-entry, queued callbacks and retired-context reuse.
    - Prove CPU blocks survive executable reload and retire once; verify targeted cache deletion.
  ☐ Completion: no delayed registration dependency remains; lifecycle checks establish coherent
    readiness, no owner resurrection and correct borrower/resource retirement ordering.

Phase 6: Reconcile documentation and final acceptance
  Depends on Phase 5. Outcome: the migration is documented and qualified at each required evidence level.
  Proposal: Compatibility, generators and documentation; Dependency-ordered implementation and acceptance.

  ☐ Update GpuProgram.md, GPU.ShaderDemandLoading.md, Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md and engine/pipeline
    boundary documentation, including Rendering.EngineStateSwitching.md where affected.
    - Describe IGpuProgram default dispatch, concrete owner state, GpuComputeProgram naming,
      registry/reload contracts, binary preparation and supported integration adapters.
    - Document intentional GpuProgram/VgeShaderProgram loss of engine assignability/inherited APIs
      and supported replacements. Reconcile obsolete memory-registration/source-compilation claims.
    - Remove remaining temporary migration paths; keep historical evidence clearly distinguished.
  ☐ Through subagents, run appropriate builds and focused pipeline, binding, sampler-retirement,
    state-switching, generator, demand-loading and resource regression suites after integration.
    - Verify generated/offline shells and packaged shader inputs through the existing build path;
      distinguish package verification from deployment. No performance claim without measurements.
    - Record failures/skips and evidence limits; expand checks only for unresolved concerns.
  ☐ Obtain user-run game acceptance: cold/warm startup, enter/leave/re-enter world, native reload,
    live shader configuration, visible/depth liquids and native rendering around VGE passes.
    - Confirm the original cached final-display startup scenario no longer fails. Do not substitute
      headless rendering or compilation for live appearance/lifecycle acceptance.
  ☐ Perform a second implementation review against every proposal section and independently audit
    completion using fresh receipts; reconcile discrepancies before marking phases complete.
  ☐ Completion: required structural, unit, GPU, generated/package and user-run receipts are linked;
    intentional limits/deferred decisions are explicit and no required acceptance is silently waived.

Final acceptance and proposal traceability
  ☐ Shared interface/default methods and compute rename: Phases 1-2; final dispatch/generator checks in 6.
  ☐ Engine independence and cached final-display fix: Phases 3-4; cold/warm user acceptance in 6.
  ☐ Existing scopes, native handoff and all graphics/compute restoration combinations: Phase 3.
  ☐ Liquid bridge, engine traversal/finally behavior and unaffected native pools: Phases 3 and 6.
  ☐ Transactional generations, stale PSO rejection, diagnostics and native/CPU ownership: Phases 4-5.
  ☐ Concrete registry, coherent reload, callback withdrawal and context retirement: Phase 5.
  ☐ Compatibility, native hooks, generators, documentation and complete validation: Phases 2-6.
  ☐ Every proposal requirement is implemented or has an explicit reviewed deferral with rationale;
    required live acceptance remains open until supplied. All temporary compatibility paths are removed.
